            rack roundup (updated August 2026) — all checked on 16 October 2026. Where we repeat
            what a reviewer found, we say so.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the four rack types that actually change the decision: a full
            four-post power rack, a half rack, a squat stand and a folding wall-mounted rack. For
            each we wanted at least one option on sale now under that name on its maker's own
            store, with upright size, steel gauge, hole spacing, footprint, safety system and
            bolting requirement that an independent review confirms. We also wanted a spread of
            prices, from a budget rack under $400 to configurable 3x3 racks well above $1,000.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where the sources disagreed we left the figure out. That happened more often than we
            expected. REP lists the PR-5000 with 2-inch hole spacing throughout, while one review
            describes Westside spacing; REP lists two upright heights where a review lists three;
            and REP and a review give different widths. Rogue and a review give different weights
            for the R-3. Titan lists a different hole size for its squat stand than a review does.
            PRx describes a limited lifetime warranty on the Profile PRO while a review describes
            a 10-year warranty. None of those figures appear on the cards. If one of them matters
            to you, check it on the maker's page for the exact configuration you are buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some well-regarded racks fell out. Bells of Steel's Hydra rack is highly rated by
            independent reviewers, but its build-your-own pricing made it hard to give a fair
            like-for-like range, and the PR-5000 covers the same configurable 3x3 role here.
            Sorinex makes heavier racks that we did not take further on this pass. Very cheap
            imported cages sold only through marketplace listings were excluded because we could
            not confirm their specifications at the maker.
          </p>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for every
            brand on this page. We found no recall covering any rack listed. We did find three
            past recalls of rack accessories from these makers: a 2011 Rogue recall of J-cup
            brackets used on R-3 and other racks of that era, a 2018 Titan recall of weightlifting
            safety straps (including T-3 sizes), and a 2021 REP recall of strap safety brackets
            for the PR-4000 and PR-5000. None covers the racks as sold today, but they are a good
