        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best squat rack for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most garage or basement gyms, a 3x3 or 2x3 11-gauge power rack with safeties included or added, such as the REP PR-5000 or Rogue R-3 (or, on Amazon, Titan's T-3 Series), set up as the maker specifies. If you cannot bolt into the floor, a half rack like the Rogue HR-2 or Titan's X-3 Series is a strong alternative. Low ceilings and small budgets point to a squat stand with spotter arms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 3x3 or 2x3 power rack more stable?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A 3x3 tube is stiffer than a 2x3 tube of the same gauge, but stability depends more on the base than the uprights. A deep footprint, bolting or a stabiliser where the maker requires it, plates stored low and a firm level floor matter more. The main practical advantage of 3x3 is a larger range of compatible attachments.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much ceiling height do I need for a squat rack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Measure to the lowest beam, light or door track and subtract any flooring, then compare that with the rack's overall height and allow room to stand the uprights during assembly. For pull-ups you need clearance above the bar for your head and shoulders, so a tall rack under a low ceiling may not allow them. Short racks and stands exist for low rooms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I have to bolt my squat rack to the floor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if the maker says so, and then you must. Some racks must be bolted, some need bolting only with certain attachments, some are designed to stand freely, and some cannot be bolted at all. Follow the manual for your exact rack and use the fixings it specifies, into a sound concrete slab where anchoring is required.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I put a power rack upstairs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Possibly, but check the structure first. A loaded rack and plate storage put a heavy, concentrated load on the floor, and dropped weights add impact. Ask a structural engineer or experienced builder to assess the floor before installing a rack upstairs, especially in an old or altered building, use dense rubber flooring, and avoid dropping weights.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are safety bars necessary on a squat rack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, for anyone squatting or benching without a spotter. J-cups hold the bar between sets; they are not designed to catch a missed lift. Pin-and-pipe safeties, strap safeties or spotter arms set just below the bottom of each lift are what make solo barbell training reasonably safe. Several racks sell them separately, so budget for them.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

